## Summary

Reading the complete frontend and route changes at `e0781fad9`, the full new regression file, and the Agent schedule parser/update symbols, this fixes the reported round trip at the correct two boundaries. The form now prefers canonical stored schedule values, while the HTTP route converts an authoritative parser rejection into a client error. I found no product-code blocker.

## Code reference

The shared form helper at `static/panels.js:490-503` matches the stored Agent shapes:

```javascript
const s = job && job.schedule;
if (s) {
  if (typeof s.run_at === "string" && s.run_at) return s.run_at;
  if (typeof s.expr === "string" && s.expr) return s.expr;
}
return (job && job.schedule_display) || "";
```

Both `duplicateCurrentCron()` at `static/panels.js:1529-1546` and `openCronEdit()` at `static/panels.js:1590-1607` use this helper, so the fix is not limited to one entry point. The Agent contract agrees: `cron/jobs.py:694` is the canonical parser, and `cron/jobs.py:2199` reparses a changed schedule during `update_job()`.

The backend guard at `api/routes.py:24735-24750` is also correctly narrow. It catches only `ValueError` from `update_job()`, preserves the existing 404 for a missing job, and leaves unexpected failures visible as server errors.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

No change requested. Keeping `schedule_display` as the last fallback is reasonable for legacy or interval-shaped records whose display text is still accepted by the parser. The important one-shot and cron shapes now avoid that fallback.

## Test plan / verification

`tests/test_issue7352_cron_once_edit_roundtrip.py:58-146` exercises the actual JavaScript helper and both form callers for one-shot and cron records. Lines 192-254 pin invalid input as 400, missing jobs as 404, and a valid one-shot update as 200. The assertions distinguish `run_at` and `expr` from display text, so they cover the original failure rather than only checking source presence.

No contributor-authored code was executed during this read-only review. Verdict: merge-ready on code inspection, subject to hosted CI.

## Summary

Reading the complete PR diff and the full changed files at `25b37645a`, I found one approval-integrity blocker in the new X-dismiss path. The backend normalization at `api/route_approvals.py:367-381` is sensible: every reconciled entry gets a stable identifier. The frontend fallback for a card that still has no identifier, however, sends a destructive deny without any target identity.

## Code reference

At `static/messages.js:7675-7703`, the request is gated only on the session id and API availability:

```javascript
if (sid && typeof api === "function") {
  const body = {session_id: sid, choice: "deny"};
  if (approvalId) body.approval_id = approvalId;
  if (owner && owner.runId) body.run_id = owner.runId;
  if (owner && owner.mirrorToken) body.mirror_token = owner.mirrorToken;
  api("/api/approval/respond", {
    method: "POST",
```

That means the explicit unresolved state added at `static/messages.js:7655-7666` can still call the response endpoint with only `{session_id, choice}`. This conflicts with the queue identity contract. `api/routes.py:26335-26359` accepts the response payload at the existing approval boundary, and `tests/test_approval_queue.py:58-62` documents the backward-compatible no-id behavior as selecting the oldest queue entry. The new static test at `tests/test_issue7242_approval_flyout_dismiss.py:174-190` checks only that the endpoint and deny choice exist; it does not require an identifier.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

If an idless card is visible and the queue advances before the X request arrives, the no-id request can deny the new head rather than the card the user dismissed. This is exactly the class of cross-entry ownership error that the current approval-id and mirror-token machinery is meant to prevent.

Please send the server-side deny only when `approvalId` is present. For a truly idless unresolved card, keep the local dismissal authoritative and let the next pending reconciliation mint and return a stable id. If server settlement of that exact legacy item is required, add a dedicated server operation bound to a non-forgeable snapshot identity rather than using FIFO fallback.

## Test plan

Add a behavior test with two queued approvals: render an idless card for the first item, advance the queue to the second, click X, and assert that no response request without `approval_id` is emitted and neither producer is denied. Keep a separate identified-card case proving that X sends the exact id, run id, and mirror token. No PR-authored code was executed during this read-only review.

## Summary

Reading the full `static/ui.js` anchor helpers on `origin/master` and the PR diff, this is the right layer for the desktop drift. The existing render-time restore finishes before the deferred post-process, so extending the same semantic row anchor across that synchronous pass closes the uncovered interval without changing the Android native-anchor path. I did not find a blocking issue in the diff.

## Code reference

`static/ui.js:19408-19429` applies three important ownership gates before creating the hold: touch-like viewports return early, readers within 250 px of the tail return early, and a scroll-input generation change cancels the eventual restore. The integration at `static/ui.js:19431-19452` also has the correct ordering:

```js
const holdAnchor=(typeof _beginPostProcessAnchorHold==="function")
  ? _beginPostProcessAnchorHold(scroller) : null;
try{
  postProcessRenderedMessages(container);
}finally{
  if(holdAnchor){ try{ holdAnchor(); }catch(_){ } }
```

That captures before `highlightCode()`, copy-button insertion, and inline artifact hydration, then restores after their synchronous layout changes. It also reuses `_captureMessageViewportAnchor()` and `_restoreMessageViewportAnchor()` (`static/ui.js:937-1138`) rather than introducing another row-identity scheme, so content-derived key fallback and stable session-index recovery stay consistent with normal render restoration.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

The scope is appropriately narrow. The code does not claim to compensate later image decode or asynchronous Mermaid/KaTeX growth, and it avoids holding tail followers to a row when the bottom pin should own scrolling. Synchronizing `_lastScrollTop` and `_lastMessageClientHeight` after a successful restore is also necessary to avoid turning this programmatic correction into a false unpin.

One non-blocking test improvement: the harness currently stubs `_suppressBrowserOverflowAnchor()` to return `null`. Add one ordering case with a non-null release callback and assert that the desktop hold runs before release. That pins the `finally` ordering called out in the implementation and protects against a future refactor moving release ahead of the restore.

## Verification

`tests/test_issue500_message_list_virtualization.py:1245-1324` covers the primary 64 px compensation, touch inertness, tail-following inertness, and reader-input cancellation using the real capture/restore helpers. Those assertions match the intended ownership rules and the modified call path.

## Summary

Reading the full two-file diff at `5c21e2db905a30cbe0916f2760414dbb4cd03ffa`, the complete regression file, and the surrounding edit/regenerate implementation on `origin/master`, the function-level guard is placed correctly. It closes the synchronous re-entry window before the first await, preserves the #2184 absolute-index capture order, and releases on every return or exception. I do not see a correctness defect in the implementation. The remaining gap is that the new test proves source shape rather than the destructive behavior it is meant to protect.

## Code reference

The important ordering is at `static/ui.js:19324-19331`:

```js
async function submitEdit(msgIdx, newText) {
  if(!S.session || S.busy || _submitEditInFlight) return;
  _submitEditInFlight = true;
  try {
    const initialSid = S.session.session_id;
    const absoluteKeepCount = _oldestIdx + msgIdx;
```

Because JavaScript runs through the assignment before yielding, a second click or Enter submission cannot reach `_ensureAllMessagesLoaded()` or the destructive history-cut POST. The outer `finally` at `static/ui.js:19356-19358` also covers both session-switch early returns and the nested request/send catch, so the page cannot remain permanently locked.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

The state transition is sound, but `tests/test_issue_edit_regenerate_absolute_keep_count.py:44-93` only searches for strings and relative positions. It would still pass if those strings moved into a dead branch or if a later refactor allowed two real calls to reach the API through another path.

Add one small Node behavioral harness around the extracted real `submitEdit()` function. Start the first call with `_ensureAllMessagesLoaded()` held on a deferred promise, invoke `submitEdit()` again, and assert only one load and one destructive POST occur. Then reject or release the first call and invoke a third time to prove the `finally` reopened the gate. That directly pins both the exclusion and recovery contracts.

## Verification

The existing #2184 tests still assert that `absoluteKeepCount` is captured before any await and sent unchanged. `regenerateResponse()` remains outside this destructive client-side path and still delegates to atomic regeneration. This was read-only worktree inspection; contributor-authored code was not executed.

## Summary

I re-read the complete `static/ui.js` at current head `57a75e8b2` and on `origin/master`, the footer stage CSS, and the complete mobile-layout regression file. I also compared the previously reviewed tree at `17bcf37bb` with this head; the `static/ui.js` path diff is empty. The rebase therefore preserves the implementation already reviewed. The height freeze, visibility suppression, stage selection, and `finally` restoration still form a coherent same-task boundary, and I found no new correctness blocker.

## Code reference

The critical ordering remains at `static/ui.js:5073-5101`:

```javascript
const frozenHeight=footer.getBoundingClientRect().height;
if(frozenHeight>0){
  footer.style.height=frozenHeight+'px';
  footer.style.visibility='hidden';
}
let next='';
try{
```

The corresponding `finally` restores the resolved `cf-icons` and `cf-burger` classes before putting the prior inline height and visibility back. That order matters: releasing the frozen box first would reintroduce an observable intermediate stage.

The layout contract still supports this. `static/style.css:2598` defines the footer as a flex container with no height transition, while `static/style.css:3009-3039` owns the two compact-stage geometries. The patch does not duplicate those rules; it only prevents their temporary removal from changing the messages viewport while overflow is measured.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

No additional product-code change is requested from this re-review. The helper preserves prior caller-owned inline styles, skips the freeze when the measured height is zero, and restores state after exceptions. Since `_scheduleComposerFooterFit()` calls the helper from a queued animation frame (`static/ui.js:5105-5112`), all destructive class probes still happen inside one callback rather than being exposed across frames.

The remaining test gap from the first review is unchanged. `tests/test_mobile_layout.py:354-367` checks only that the helper measures widths and mentions both class names. It does not execute the function or prove that height, visibility, and starting-stage state are restored. A focused DOM behavior test should start from full, icons, and burger stages, force each overflow outcome, and assert the footer border-box height and prior inline styles remain stable through the probe.

## Verification

Current exact-head GitHub metadata shows test, lint, browser-smoke, and all three lifecycle checks green. The implementation tree is unchanged from the prior review. No contributor-authored code was executed during this read-only re-review.

## Summary

Reading the complete two-file diff at `5c509fb9e`, the cron poller and notification owner on `origin/master`, the full new behavioral harness, and the recent-completions projection, the hidden-tab polling design is sound but one blocking delivery gap remains. A completion without a matched cron session is consumed without a notification. This is not an invalid response shape: the backend deliberately emits an empty `session_id` when no matching persisted cron session can be found.

## Code reference

The new hidden path at `static/panels.js:12991-13001` gates notification delivery on the optional session link:

```js
if(completionHidden){
  const completionStatus=t("cron_completion_status", c.name, c.status==="error" ? t("status_failed") : t("status_completed"));
  if(c.session_id){
    sendBrowserNotification(c.name||t("untitled"),completionStatus,{forceHidden:true,sid:c.session_id});
  }
}
_cronPollSince=Math.max(_cronPollSince,c.completed_at);
```

The backend contract at `api/routes.py:21931-21948` first creates a completion from job state, then attaches session metadata on a best-effort basis. `_latest_cron_session_info_for_jobs()` returns `session_id: ""` when the active state database is absent, unavailable, or has no matching `cron_<job>_...` row (`api/routes.py:307-380`). Such a completion is still valid and still carries job name, status, timestamp, and notification preference.

The browser notification owner already supports this case. `_notificationOptions()` at `static/messages.js:9147-9150` falls back to the current page URL and the generic WebUI tag when no session id exists. Requiring `sid` at the call site is therefore stricter than the owner contract.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

This branch advances `_cronPollSince` after skipping the alert, so the omission is permanent. The new no-session regression also codifies that loss instead of exposing it.

Call the notification owner for every enabled hidden completion and add `sid` only when present:

```js
const options={forceHidden:true};
if(c.session_id) options.sid=c.session_id;
sendBrowserNotification(c.name||t("untitled"),completionStatus,options);
```

That keeps session-aware click-through where available while preserving the actual job-completion notification when linkage is unavailable.

## Verification

Change the no-session behavioral case to expect one notification, an options object containing only `forceHidden`, and the same cursor/unread advancement. Keep the current visible, muted, permission, stale-generation, rejected-poll, and overlap controls. This was read-only worktree inspection; I did not execute contributor-authored code.

## Summary

Reading the new custom-model routes, the discovery and inference implementations, the endpoint probe, the existing authentication dispatcher, and the local-origin security tests on `origin/master`, this has a merge-blocking passwordless boundary gap. The new discovery and inference-test endpoints can make server-side requests to private or loopback targets and can reuse the active profile stored credential, but route dispatch does not apply the established local-origin gate when WebUI authentication is disabled.

## Code reference

`server.py:399-410` calls `check_auth()` before every write route. `api/auth.py:1075-1079` then admits every request when authentication is disabled:

```python
def check_auth(handler, parsed) -> bool:
    if not is_auth_enabled():
        return True
```

The PR registers both network-capable actions at `api/routes.py:15540-15564` and calls them directly under a profile environment wrapper:

```python
if parsed.path in {
    "/api/providers/custom-models/test",
    "/api/providers/custom-models/discover",
}:
    with profile_env_for_active_request_readonly(parsed.path, logger_override=logger):
        if parsed.path.endswith("/discover"):
            return j(handler, discover_custom_models(body))
        return j(handler, test_custom_model(body))
```

That wrapper scopes credentials but does not authorize the caller. `api/custom_models.py:1231-1286` accepts a private or loopback `base_url`, resolves a stored entry by `uid`, loads its stored key, and sends the models request. The inference path at `api/custom_models.py:1296-1430` similarly loads the stored key and performs a POST. `api/provider_endpoint_probe.py:1-12` explicitly permits private and loopback destinations, which is correct for self-hosted providers only if the caller boundary is enforced.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

This creates a server-side request and credential-forwarding primitive on a remotely reachable passwordless deployment. A remote caller can enumerate a configured custom entry, select its `uid`, and make the WebUI contact that stored endpoint with the active profile credential. Even without response-body disclosure, this can drive requests to internal services and consume provider credentials or quota.

Apply a gate before either network action, using the same trust model already established for the embedded terminal at `api/routes.py:6108-6204`. A narrow route shape is:

```python
if parsed.path in {test_path, discover_path}:
    if not _onboarding_gate_allows(handler):
        return bad(handler, "Provider endpoint tests require a local client when authentication is disabled.", 403)
    # enter the profile-scoped read context and perform the action
```

Keep CRUD and list behavior separate from this finding; the blocker is the two routes that originate network traffic and may reuse stored credentials.

## Verification

Add production-route tests for auth-disabled public clients, loopback/private clients, auth-enabled clients, and spoofed forwarded headers. The public cases must return 403 before `discover_custom_models()` or `test_custom_model()` is called. Include a stored-`uid` case proving no credential lookup or opener call occurs after denial. The current custom-model tests exercise function behavior and route string presence, but do not test this caller boundary. This was read-only worktree inspection; I did not execute contributor-authored code.

## Summary

Reading the current `a75ef98d9` head, the shared response gate in `static/ui.js:19619-19631` now preserves HTTP status without exposing the response body, and Patch, CSV, Excalidraw, PDF, and HTML all route their fetch failures through it. The follow-up commit also resolves the two concerns raised against the first revision: 403 copy is no longer allowlist-specific, and every changed loader now has behavioral rather than source-membership coverage. I do not see a remaining code blocker in this scope.

## Code reference

The status-carrying boundary is small and keeps non-HTTP failures on each format fallback:

```js
function _requireMediaResponse(response){
  if(response.ok) return response;
  const error=new Error(String(response.status));
  error.status=response.status;
  throw error;
}
```

That distinction matches the backend contract. `api/routes.py:20623-20629` uses 403 for hard-deny and allowlist rejection, while `api/routes.py:19495-19506` can also return 403 for filesystem permission or anchored-open failures. The revised English string at `static/i18n.js:72-74` accurately covers both location and permission cases instead of asserting that every 403 is outside the workspace.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

The implementation is appropriately narrow. `static/ui.js:19639-19985` preserves existing format-specific handling for network, parse, renderer, 5xx, and unexpected failures because those errors have no recognized status. It changes only 401, 403, and 404. That avoids parsing raw backend details and does not alter `/api/media` authorization.

The added test shape is materially stronger at current HEAD. `tests/test_media_preview_http_errors.py:74-113` checks the generalized 403 copy and the reported Patch path. More importantly, `tests/test_media_preview_http_errors.py:142-200` parameterizes all five loaders, supplies a 403 response, and asserts the rendered recovery text while rejecting the old format fallback. This now exercises the changed catches rather than only searching their source.

## Verification

This was a read-only review of the full diff and the relevant backend route; I did not execute code from the PR worktree. GitHub checks for `a75ef98d9` were still running when inspected, so merge should remain conditional on that final check set completing successfully.

## Summary

Reading `static/workspace.js:441-505`, `static/workspace.js:640-727`, and both artifact test files at `09fb70cb4`, the fail-closed rendering and direct-opener guard are sound for the covered paths. There is one blocking normalization gap: an absolute path contained in the workspace but carrying a repeated separator is classified as actionable with an `openPath` that begins with `/`. The later workspace route treats that as invalid and substitutes the workspace root, so the row can open the wrong target or fail despite being classified as contained.

## Code reference

The empty path segment is retained when the absolute path is split, then reused as the open path:

```js
segments:(match ? match[2] : value).split('/').filter(segment => segment !== '.'),
// ...
const suffix = pathParts.segments.slice(workspaceParts.segments.length).join('/');
const dedupePath = suffix || '.';
return {kind:'workspace-contained', displayPath:dedupePath, dedupeKey:pathIsWindows ? dedupePath.toLowerCase() : dedupePath, openPath:dedupePath};
```

For `/workspace//report.md` under workspace `/workspace`, `static/workspace.js:482-501` produces the suffix `/report.md`. `openArtifactPath()` accepts the `workspace-contained` kind at `static/workspace.js:700-726`; `_workspacePathExists()` can still match the root entry by filename at lines 690-697. It then passes `/report.md` to `openFile()`. But `_normalizeWorkspaceRelPath()` explicitly returns an empty string for leading `/` at `static/workspace.js:166-180`, and `_workspaceRouteForPathRel()` falls back to `.` at lines 261-277. The same shape is possible with repeated separators in a Windows absolute path.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

Please separate display preservation from canonical open/deduplication. A minimal shape near `static/workspace.js:499` is:

```js
const displayPath = suffix || '.';
const openPath = suffix.split('/').filter(Boolean).join('/') || '.';
return {
  kind:'workspace-contained', displayPath,
  dedupeKey:pathIsWindows ? openPath.toLowerCase() : openPath,
  openPath,
};
```

That keeps repeated separators visible if the UI contract requires it, while ensuring the path sent to workspace APIs is relative and aliases dedupe with `report.md`.

## Test plan

Add POSIX and Windows absolute repeated-separator rows to `tests/test_issue7239_artifact_workspace_boundary.py:70-132`, asserting `openPath === "report.md"` and dedupe parity with the relative alias. Also extend the direct-opener case at lines 134-152 to verify the list request and final open target. `tests/test_issue6067_artifact_paths.py:28` covers repeated separators only in a relative display path, so it does not catch this absolute-boundary failure. This is a read-only review; I did not execute code from the PR worktree.

@nesquena This is ready for maintainer review.

I rebased it onto current `master` and re-ran an extra independent audit before requesting review. That audit found a real same-target replacement race: an old async invocation could settle after unregister/re-register and clear the replacement registration's pending state. The current head `04b88f793144406d2c3e6eeed8033158f7847ad4` fixes it with per-invocation identity and adds a mutation-checked regression.

Exact-head status: 23 GitHub checks successful, 0 failing, 0 pending; the focused local gate is 228 passed, 7 skipped. The PR body now contains the exact commands and run links.

Review feedback addressed on the current head:

- stale/blank Resource Timing no longer walks back to old h2/h3 evidence;
- newest same-origin resource evidence overrides navigation timing, with navigation used only as fallback;
- no page-lifetime protocol cache remains;
- reconnect, SSE error, online transition, or BFCache restore locks the pool to the HTTP/1.1-safe budget (3) until reload and prunes immediately;
- safe pruning preserves the foreground stream via `S.session.session_id`, covered by a dynamic four-stream test;
- the runtime contract is now documented in `docs/sse-streams.md`.

Focused frontend regression set: 97 passed; the full GitHub Actions matrix, browser smoke, lint, and conversation-cycle checks are green.

Round-3 re-gate addressed — both deeper CORE gaps are closed:

**1. Occurrence-count collision** — the guard now refuses the bounded path whenever ANY bounded-tail visible key also occurs in the skipped prefix (conservative fallback to full read). This covers the repeated-prompt case: a new state.db tail turn repeating an older prompt whose key is below the floor can no longer be mistaken for the old sidecar duplicate and dropped.

**2. TOCTOU — proof and data are now one snapshot.** New `get_state_db_regeneration_tail_snapshot()` (api/models.py) resolves the DB **once** and performs the prefix summary, the ordered prefix keys, AND the bounded tail query inside a **single read transaction** (BEGIN…COMMIT on one connection). `regeneration_state(use_sidecar=True)` consumes the tail from that same snapshot — it never re-reads through a second connection. If a stable snapshot cannot be obtained, the guard returns None and the caller falls back to the full read (no window).

**Tests added (29 passed in test_issue6611_regeneration_authority, +40 with stale/recovery neighbors):**
- `test_bounded_guard_repeated_tail_key_falls_back` — repeated key below+above the floor forces full read;
- `test_bounded_guard_missing_snapshot_falls_back` — unstable snapshot refuses the bounded path entirely;
- `test_regeneration_tail_snapshot_reads_real_sqlite` — real-SQLite mint→validate of the single-connection snapshot, asserting the repeated-prompt key appears in BOTH prefix_keys and tail_keys (the exact collision shape);
- updated `test_regeneration_sidecar_path_avoids_full_transcript_refetch` to prove the fast path never touches the full reader (tail comes from the snapshot).

Ready for re-gate.

## Re-gate: the remaining cross-approval path is now closed

Verified against the new commit `0a9a5ec3d` (pushed after my last re-gate). The one path I flagged last round — the capture reading the mutable pending map, so a same-session successor B that renders mid-await could still be cross-approved — is closed. Nice work @BBaFSE.

## What I verified in the diff

The click-time snapshot now sources the RENDERED card, never the map. `_captureApprovalResponseOwner` at `static/messages.js:7524` bails unless `_approvalDisplayedOwner` matches the currently-visible `sid`/`approvalId`, and returns a copy of that render-time owner:

```js
if (
  !_approvalDisplayedOwner ||
  _approvalDisplayedOwner.sid !== sid ||
  _approvalDisplayedOwner.approvalId !== approvalId
) return null;
return {..._approvalDisplayedOwner, generation: _loadSessionGeneration};
```

`_approvalDisplayedOwner.displayedKey` is set only inside `showApprovalCard` (`static/messages.js:7645`), after the `_approvalPromptBelongsToActiveSession` and `_isApprovalDismissed` early returns — so a suppressed successor that never rendered can't leak its key into the capture.

The reconcile then compares the head against that captured render-time key, and refuses to fall back to the map when an owner was captured (`static/messages.js:7999`):

```js
let displayedKey = null;
if (owner && owner.displayedKey) {
  displayedKey = owner.displayedKey;
} else if (!owner) {
  displayedKey = _approvalLogicalKey(sid, pendingEntry && pendingEntry.pending);
}
const sameLogical = !!(headKey && head.approval_id && displayedKey && headKey === displayedKey);
```

Critically, even on a `sameLogical` match the adopt is gated by `stillOnSession && (!owner || _approvalResponseOwnerIsCurrent(owner))` (`static/messages.js:8017`), and `_approvalResponseOwnerIsCurrent` re-checks `_approvalOwnerIdentityMatches(_approvalDisplayedOwner, owner)` plus the load generation. So if a newer render (successor B) took over the card under the await, the owner is no longer current, the branch fails closed, and no adopt happens.

## Test coverage matches the closed path

The two new sandbox cases pin exactly this: `test_click_on_a_never_resolves_same_session_successor_b` (A's click must not POST when a same-session successor B rendered mid-GET) and `test_capture_sources_rendered_card_not_map_with_dismissed_successor` (capture must ignore a dismissed successor sitting in the map). `test_reconcile_await_session_switch_blocks_stale_respond_and_dismiss` and `test_reconcile_does_not_hide_newer_session_card_on_switch` cover the session-switch-under-await cases my earlier gate worried about. `test_gateway_run_unavailable_keeps_live_successor` confirms a live successor isn't hidden/cleared as an orphan.

## Verdict

From a static read this closes the invariant: a response id is reconciled to the server head at click time, and a click is never applied to a different logical approval. I can't run the browser gate from here (I don't execute PR code), so the headless `tests/browser_approval_first_click.py` run is on CI/maintainer. Greptile is green; my `CHANGES_REQUESTED` concern is resolved on inspection. Recommend a maintainer confirm the browser gate passes, then this is good to merge.

## Summary

I read the four-file patch at head `2a5d097e`, including the complete new regression file and the compaction, preservation, and virtual-window contexts in `renderMessages()` against `origin/master`. The digest extraction and settled-summary top pin are reasonable. The loaded-marker path still drops every compaction card whose marker is before the current virtual render window, and it can drop the preserved task cards with it. That directly affects the long transcripts this PR is intended to repair.

## Code reference

`renderMessages()` scans all loaded messages and creates nodes for every marker at `static/ui.js:16753-16768`. It then marks the preserved task cards as attached whenever that array is nonempty at `static/ui.js:16769-16773`.

Insertion later rejects pre-window markers without placing their nodes anywhere (`static/ui.js:17382-17399`):

```js
function _insertCompressionLikeNodeByRawIdx(node, rawIdx){
  if(!node) return;
  if(rawIdx<firstRenderedRawIdx) return;
  if(!renderVisWithIdx.length){
    inner.appendChild(node);
    return;
  }
```

For a virtualized transcript, `_messageVirtualWindow()` at `static/ui.js:644-691` can make `firstRenderedRawIdx` much later than a loaded compaction marker. The marker node is then discarded. Because `preservedCompressionTaskCardsAttached` was already set from `compactionCardNodes.length`, the standalone fallback at `static/ui.js:17422-17426` is suppressed too. The result can be no compaction card and no preserved tasks.

The test at `tests/test_compaction_card_display.py:106-125` covers only `referenceNode` with `referenceMessageRawIdx === -1`. It does not exercise a loaded marker with a valid raw index before a virtual window, which is the failing branch above.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

Separate discovered markers from markers that can actually be inserted. Keep inline cards for markers at or after `firstRenderedRawIdx`. If one or more loaded markers precede the window, pin the latest pre-window digest at the top, similar to the settled-summary fallback, rather than creating and then discarding all of them. Attach preserved tasks to the latest card that is actually inserted; only suppress `preservedOnlyNode` after insertion ownership is known.

## Verification step

Add an execution-level `renderMessages()` case with more than the virtualization threshold, a compaction marker before `virtualWindow.start`, and preserved tasks. Assert that one pre-window digest is visible above the rendered rows, the task cards appear exactly once, and in-window markers remain positioned inline. I did not execute PR-authored code during this read-only review.

