## Summary

I reviewed current head `5b6d4bba` against `origin/master`, including the full `static/ui.js`, `static/sessions.js`, and the 21-case multi-tab regression file. The latest push fixes the prior lease-expiry, malformed-registry GC, legacy-session adoption, and inflight-migration findings. One core race remains: `_claimTabOwnership()` describes its localStorage sequence as compare-and-swap, but localStorage has no compare-and-swap or transaction spanning the read, write, and verification. Two simultaneous duplicate-tab claimers can still both return success for the same tab ID.

## Code reference

At `static/ui.js:9369-9382`, ownership is claimed with three separate storage operations:

```js
const reg=_readTabClaims();
reg[id]={nonce, ts:Date.now(), released:false};
if(!_writeTabClaims(reg)) return false;
const check=_readTabClaims();
const mine=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(check,id)?check[id]:null;
return !!(mine&&mine.nonce===nonce&&!mine.released);
```

This permits the following interleaving: A and B both read the ID as free; A writes and verifies A, then returns success; B writes and verifies B, then also returns success. A has already accepted the same ID even though B now owns its registry record. The five-second early return in `_revalidateTabOwnership()` at `static/ui.js:9414-9418` then leaves a window in which A can read or write the shared `::<tabId>` keys before noticing the overwrite.

The tests do not exercise this schedule. `tests/test_multi_tab_session_isolation.py:152-166` creates the two tabs sequentially, so B sees A before attempting its claim. The registry test at lines 338-373 also performs completed operations in sequence and never pauses two claimers after their initial reads.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

Please do not call this authoritative until arbitration uses a real exclusion primitive. A shared Web Lock around the claim transaction is the direct browser primitive, though making synchronous storage helpers await boot-time lock acquisition will require some restructuring. Another valid design is an identity source that is inherently document-unique and does not need a shared synchronous claim registry. A later storage event or throttled revalidation is only recovery after contamination has already become possible.

## Verification

Add a deterministic same-ID claim race: pause A after its first `_readTabClaims()`, let B complete and return success, then resume A. Assert that at most one caller returns the inherited ID and that the loser remints before any active-session or inflight-key access. The current implementation will let both report success under that schedule. I did not execute PR-authored code during this read-only review.

