Reading the full `static/ui.js` and `static/style.css` files on origin/master and PR HEAD, plus the complete new regression test, the gesture math and resize cleanup are internally consistent. I found one blocking interaction regression: clicking the empty area around a fitted image no longer closes the lightbox. The new fixed-size viewport occupies 90% of the screen and stops every click, so the backdrop handler is unreachable over most of the modal.

## Code reference

The PR makes the viewport a large clipping surface at `static/style.css:2457-2459`:

```css
.img-lightbox-viewport{position:relative;width:90vw;height:90vh;overflow:hidden;border-radius:8px;touch-action:none;user-select:none;-webkit-user-select:none;cursor:grab;}
.img-lightbox-viewport.is-panning{cursor:grabbing;}
.img-lightbox-canvas{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;transform-origin:0 0;will-change:transform;}
```

Then `static/ui.js:2509-2520` suppresses bubbling unconditionally:

```js
function _onViewportClick(e) {
  if(state.dragged){
    state.dragged = false;
  }
  if(e.stopPropagation) e.stopPropagation();
}
```

The lightbox still installs its close-on-backdrop handler at `static/ui.js:2662`, but that handler now runs only in the outer 5% border beyond the viewport. On origin/master at `static/ui.js:2379-2420`, only the image stopped propagation, so all surrounding blank space closed the dialog. This is a noticeable regression for small or portrait images with large letterboxed areas.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

Keep clicks on the transformed canvas from closing, and keep the post-drag click suppression, but allow an undragged click whose target is the viewport itself to bubble to the existing lightbox handler:

```js
function _onViewportClick(e) {
  const wasDragged=state.dragged;
  state.dragged=false;
  if(wasDragged || e.target!==viewport) e.stopPropagation();
}
```

Because the image has `pointer-events:none` and the natural-size canvas is the transformed hit target, clicks on visible image pixels reach the canvas, while clicks in the letterboxed viewport area target the viewport. This preserves image clicks, pan completion, and backdrop dismissal without adding another close path.

## Test plan

The new `tests/test_img_lightbox_pinch_zoom.py:22-43` checks source structure but does not assert click behavior. Add a behavior-level case that invokes the mounted click handler with: (1) `target === viewport`, no drag, and expects propagation; (2) `target === canvas` and expects `stopPropagation`; and (3) `state.dragged === true` with viewport target and expects suppression plus reset. I did not execute PR-authored code because this review path is read-only.

