## Summary

Reading `static/boot.js:3535-3780`, the full `static/sessions.js` change, the new source-level tests, and the server validator at `api/workspace.py:809-889`, the one-shot routing shape is reasonable but it currently has two correctness gaps. I would not merge this as-is because valid WebUI workspaces can be rejected in the browser, and a compound profile/workspace launch can create a session under the wrong profile after a failed profile switch.

## Code reference

The routing block at `static/boot.js:3694-3720` consumes the parameter and immediately reuses the profile-switch workspace slot:

```javascript
if(workspaceIntent.valid){
  try{
    S._profileSwitchWorkspace=workspaceIntent.path;
    await newSession(true,{worktree:false});
```

The client predicate at `static/sessions.js:280-295` defines valid as an absolute slash-prefixed path with no `..` substring. That disagrees with the authoritative server contract. `api/workspace.py:809-889` explicitly supports cross-platform home paths, including Windows drive paths, and canonicalizes and validates the candidate through `resolve_trusted_workspace()`. The POST path reaches that validator from `api/routes.py:12443`, so the client check should not narrow the accepted path language.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

First, `C:\Users\name\project` can be a valid workspace on a Windows host but fails `path.startsWith("/")`. Conversely, a legitimate Unix directory name containing two dots also fails even though server canonicalization can safely decide it. Treat any nonblank `workspace` value as a routing candidate and leave trust, existence, directory, system-root, and saved-workspace decisions to `resolve_trusted_workspace()`. If an early client check is kept, it needs platform-aware absolute-path handling and segment-aware traversal logic rather than a blanket substring test.

