The range replacement at `static/ui.js:16108-16115` is not equivalent to the removed `renderableRawIdxs` membership set. `visWithIdx` is sorted by `rawIdx`, but it is sparse because `_getVisibleMessagesWithIdx()` filters through `_messageIsRenderable()` at `static/ui.js:640-696`. A raw index between the minimum and maximum is therefore not necessarily present in `visWithIdx`. Treating every index in that range as renderable can suppress legacy tool/thinking reconstruction for filtered assistant messages in a virtualized transcript.

## Code reference

The changed predicate is:

```js
const visRawMin=visWithIdx.length?visWithIdx[0].rawIdx:Infinity;
const visRawMax=visWithIdx.length?visWithIdx[visWithIdx.length-1].rawIdx:-1;
const _isRenderableRawIdx=(idx)=>idx>=visRawMin&&idx<=visRawMax;
```

The comment says monotonic ordering makes a range check sufficient, but monotonic does not mean contiguous. At `static/ui.js:640`, a message is only included when `_messageIsRenderable(m)` returns true. For example, an assistant row with no visible text but with tool metadata can be omitted from `visWithIdx`, while `S.toolCalls[*].assistant_msg_idx` still points to its raw index. If renderable rows exist on either side, the new predicate returns true for that missing index. The guards at `static/ui.js:16971`, `static/ui.js:16982`, and `static/ui.js:16993` then `continue`, even though the assistant row is not represented in the virtual window and the legacy activity fallback is still needed.

The previous set performed exact membership and returned false for this hole. This behavior is not covered by the updated assertion in `tests/test_issue500_message_list_virtualization.py:112-115`; that test only checks that the new source string exists. The integrated cases in `tests/test_anchor_fallback_ownership.py:337-782` run with a non-virtualized window, so they do not exercise these three guards either.

## Diagnosis / recommendation

Please keep exact membership. If avoiding the allocation is important, use binary search over sorted `visWithIdx` rather than a min/max test:

```js
const _isRenderableRawIdx=(idx)=>{
  let lo=0,hi=visWithIdx.length-1;
  while(lo<=hi){
    const mid=(lo+hi)>>>1,raw=visWithIdx[mid].rawIdx;
    if(raw===idx)return true;
    if(raw<idx)lo=mid+1;else hi=mid-1;
  }
  return false;
};
```

That preserves exact semantics with no extra full-size set and logarithmic lookup cost.

