## Review — targeted fix, traces cleanly, CI green across all 18 shards

Reading `static/ui.js` at HEAD `481362e6` against `origin/master`, the root cause of the duplicate is clear. The old dedup keyed off a single normalized-key `Set`:

```js
// origin/master ui.js:4093-4095
const _existingConfiguredKeys=new Set(_modelData.map(existing=>_normalizeConfiguredModelKey(existing.value)));
if(_existingConfiguredKeys.has(_normalizeConfiguredModelKey(modelId))) continue;
```

The trap is that `_normalizeConfiguredModelKey("@custom:example:model-a")` strips only the `@custom:` segment and keeps the rest, yielding `example:model.a` — while the bare `model-a` normalizes to `model.a`. Different keys, so both rows survived and the same model showed twice. The new `_isEquivalentConfiguredModelEntry` (ui.js:3649-3671) closes that with an explicit second pass that slices the `@provider:` prefix and compares the *routed* id:

```js
const routedId=rawId.slice(prefix.length);
return (entries||[]).some(entry=>
  String(entry.providerId||'').toLowerCase()===provider
  &&_normalizeConfiguredModelKey(entry.value)===_normalizeConfiguredModelKey(routedId)
);
```

I traced both regression cases by hand and they hold: `@custom:example:model-a` vs. an existing `{value:"model-a", providerId:"custom:example"}` → `True` (second branch, routedId `model-a` matches same-provider row); `@custom:backup:model-a` against a `custom:primary` row → `False` (provider mismatch keeps them distinct). That's exactly the behavior `test_named_custom_provider_routing_id_does_not_duplicate_picker_row` and `test_same_model_id_from_another_provider_remains_distinct` pin, and all 18 CI checks (browser-smoke, lint, py3.11–3.13 shards, Greptile) are green.

## One nuance worth a confirming test

The first branch treats an empty provider on *either* side as a wildcard match:

```js
if(matchingEntries.some(existing=>{
  const entryProvider=String(existing.providerId||'').toLowerCase();
  return !provider||!entryProvider||entryProvider===provider;   // ui.js:3656-3658
})) return true;
```

`!entryProvider` means a provider-less catalog row (the ungrouped `OPTION` rows push `providerId:''` at ui.js:4111) will absorb a badge whose provider *is* set, as long as the normalized keys match. That's almost certainly intended — a bare catalog row is the same physical model and should just receive the badge rather than spawn a second row — but it's the one place where a genuinely-distinct provider could get collapsed into a provider-less row. The current suite only exercises rows that carry an explicit `providerId`. A third case asserting a bare-id badge with a set provider dedupes against a `providerId:''` catalog row (expected `True`) would lock that wildcard semantics in so a future refactor of `_normalizeConfiguredModelKey` can't silently flip it.

Not a blocker — the fix is correct and minimal, and removing the stale `_existingConfiguredKeys` guard in the follow-up commit is clean. LGTM.

## Summary

Read the diff at HEAD against `origin/master` for `static/ui.js:12078-12122`, `api/streaming.py:7524-7543`, and `api/routes.py:3178-3212`. The direction is right and matches the invariant #6187 states — "text similarity alone must not become durable event identity." Moving prose dedup from normalized text to `source_event_type:local_id` provenance is the correct shape. Two concerns below (one already flagged by Greptile, one it didn't cover), plus a scope note against the issue's exit criteria.

## 1. `proseScopeKey` sentinel — reachability is narrower than it looks, but real

Greptile is right that `proseScopeKey` returns a truthy `":"` when both fields are empty:

```js
const proseScopeKey=(row)=>String(row.source_event_type||'').trim()+':'+String(row.local_id||'').trim();
```

Worth pinning down where this actually bites: the dedup only runs under `if(live&&row.role==='prose')`. Settled projection rows built by `_anchorSceneRowBase` (messages.js:2946) carry `local_id:null` but only render with `settled=true`, so `live=false` and they skip the gate entirely — those are safe. The exposure is strictly **live** prose rows that lack both `source_event_type` and `local_id`; any two of them collapse to key `":"` and the second is silently dropped. Live token rows normally carry a `live-prose:<stream>:N` local_id (ui.js:12345), so this is an edge, but a defensive guard is cheap:

```js
const proseScopeKey=(row)=>{
  const s=String(row.source_event_type||'').trim();
  const l=String(row.local_id||'').trim();
  return (s||l) ? `${s}:${l}` : '';   // empty → falsy → no dedup, falls back to keyFor()
};
```

Returning `''` makes both call sites' `if(scopeKey && ...)` guards no-op, so a provenance-less row falls back to the primary `keyFor()` identity (which already uses `row_id`/index) instead of colliding.

## 2. streaming.py — the 80+ char containment check narrowed silently

This is the part I'd want a test for before merge. On master, `_is_visible_output_echo` ran two checks against `visible_output = STREAM_PARTIAL_TEXT.get(stream_id, '')` — the **full** accumulated visible text:

```python
# master, api/streaming.py:7542-7543
visible_compact = _compact_for_echo_compare(visible_output)
return bool(visible_compact and candidate in visible_compact)
```

The PR repoints both checks at `_token_tail_cache[0]`, which `on_token` trims to `max(len(text_str)*2, 512)` on every emission. The suffix check is roughly preserved (it always looked at a tail), but the `candidate in visible_compact` containment check now only sees a rolling ~512-char window instead of the whole answer. A reasoning echo of a phrase emitted early in a long response — outside the current window — will no longer be classified as an echo and will render as a duplicate. Also note the window is recomputed per token, so a small token (len 3 → window 512) shrinks the cache immediately after a large token grew it. This is a genuine behavior change, not a refactor; #6187's exit criteria explicitly ask that "true echoes still render once" be held across the change.

## 3. Scope vs. #6187's exit criteria

#6187 frames the work as red-first: add the 6-case negative matrix (true echo, legitimate repeated prose across a tool boundary, reasoning+prose same text, repeated reasoning segments, retry/replay idempotence, lifecycle parity) and a **failing** test demonstrating a legitimate same-text/different-identity event currently being suppressed, *before* the product change. This PR ships the rename plus a positive assertion bump in `test_live_to_final_anchor_visible_order.py` (live count 3→4), but I don't see the negative-invariant tests the issue's exit criteria require. As Greptile notes, every test row here has an explicit `local_id`, so neither the `":"` sentinel nor the containment-window narrowing is exercised. Adding case 2 (two prose rows, same text, different `local_id`, tool boundary between) and a case that pins the full-text containment behavior would close bot

